WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.260
Today I am very happy. Do you&nbsp;
know why? Of course, because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.880
are going to talk about an incredible&nbsp;
AI light transport simulation paper.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.820
You see, these simulations normally take&nbsp;
place by creating a little virtual scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:26.940
and we would like to see what this virtual&nbsp;
scene would look like in reality. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.920
do this by using a technique called&nbsp;
ray tracing, which simulates the path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.640
of millions and millions of light rays to&nbsp;
create a beautiful image or even video. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.520
technology is also used for simulating the&nbsp;
path of soundwaves in some modern speakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:52.680
and even in Apple’s new Vision Pro headset.
So, millions of rays, right? Sounds great!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:01:00.000
However, look. This is anything but a&nbsp;
beautiful scene. Why is that? Well, before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.060
we simulate enough light rays, which may&nbsp;
take from minutes to hours, we will get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:12.600
noisy image. This image will get less and less&nbsp;
noisy over time, however, we don’t have hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:19.440
of time per image. In a video game or any kind&nbsp;
of virtual world, we prefer real time results.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.280
So, what do we do? Well, we start noise&nbsp;
filtering. These are techniques where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.140
we can show a noisy image. And,&nbsp;
magically, they clear up this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:37.500
noise by imagining what this image could&nbsp;
depict. Some of them are incredibly good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:44.520
and many of these AI-based techniques are so&nbsp;
fast that they even work for video. Loving it.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:51.720
However, not so fast. Unfortunately, if we&nbsp;
add volumetric effects like haze and smoke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:59.700
things get a lot more difficult. Look. This is the&nbsp;
noisy input with smoke. And a previous technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:08.040
does…this. Whoa. That is not great. Well,&nbsp;
do not worry, because this technique doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:14.400
yet understand smoke, just hard surfaces.&nbsp;
So let’s see what this previous technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:23.100
with additional training on volumes can do. Ouch.&nbsp;
Well, smoke looks a bit more like smoke now, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:31.200
unfortunately, the whole scene got destroyed. So,&nbsp;
is that it? We can perform incredible ray tracing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:39.000
on these beautiful scenes, but only without&nbsp;
haze smoke and similar volumetric effects?

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.620
Well, don’t despair quite yet, hold&nbsp;
on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.760
and have a look at the new technique. Wow!

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:55.140
This is way better than the previous technique.&nbsp;
Actually, it is not perfect, but so much closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:01.380
the true simulation result. This is the one that&nbsp;
would have taken up to several hours to compute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:09.540
and this is what we now get almost immediately&nbsp;
from the new AI. My goodness! And once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:15.360
this is compared to the previous method&nbsp;
that was trained on volumes too. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.480
was not just by chance, when comparing other&nbsp;
cases, the new method also comes out ahead.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:30.000
So, if the new one is so good, this previous one&nbsp;
must be from super long ago, right? Some ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.040
paper. Well, is it some ancient paper? Nope.&nbsp;
Not at all, this is the Neural Temporal Adaptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:46.380
Sampling and Denoising paper from 2020. Such&nbsp;
incredible improvement in just one paper. Wow!

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:51.900
And hold on to your papers Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars, because here comes the best part:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.900 --> 00:04:00.120
all this runs now not in hours, and not even&nbsp;
in minutes. What you see here runs in real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:06.720
time. It can really take these incredibly&nbsp;
noisy sequence of images, and my goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:14.100
it reconstructs these beautiful videos from it&nbsp;
that are closer to reality than ever before. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:19.260
absolutely love this. It almost feels like&nbsp;
we are living in a science fiction movie.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:26.640
And can that really be? Yes, it gets even&nbsp;
better. The technique decomposes these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:33.600
images into surface and volume components, so&nbsp;
it can separate the opaque objects in the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:41.520
from the smoke itself. Is that good? Well, to&nbsp;
say that is good news would be an understatement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.280
because look! It can even reimagine the&nbsp;
scene as if the lighting was different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:53.940
or we can even play with the smoke plumes&nbsp;
themselves. So, from now on, we will likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.940 --> 00:05:01.440
be able to enjoy these beautiful games with&nbsp;
simulated smoke. Bravo! What a time to be alive!

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:08.220
Also, training this neural network took only&nbsp;
24 hours on a single NVIDIA graphics card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:15.960
that is insanity, and that just needs to happen&nbsp;
once, and then, everyone in the world can enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:22.140
it for as long as they wish. And it didn’t&nbsp;
even take looking at millions and millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:29.220
of noisy and clean image pairs to train&nbsp;
this network, it only took a bit more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:36.780
10,000 images. That is not a lot. Proper learning&nbsp;
from very little data. I am out of words.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:43.140
And when watching all these beautiful results,&nbsp;
if you feel that this ray tracing thing is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:49.740
cool, and you would like to learn more about it,&nbsp;
I held a Master-level course on this topic at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:55.860
Technical University of Vienna. Since I was always&nbsp;
teaching it to a handful of motivated students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:01.140
I thought that it’s not right to have the&nbsp;
teachings only be available for the privileged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:08.220
few who can afford a college education. No. The&nbsp;
teachings should be available for everyone. Free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:14.460
education for everyone, that’s what I want.&nbsp;
So, the course is available free of charge for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:19.920
everyone, no strings attached, so make sure to&nbsp;
click the link in the video description to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:26.340
started. We write a full light simulation program&nbsp;
from scratch there, and learn about physics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.620
the world around us, and more. If you watch&nbsp;
